oc = 64: SIZE_E_2 = 1
=====================

Concretely, as you asked: **1**.

The expression, which is what the merge request emits and what I should have
sent instead of the sentence about 0x124 and 0x024:

  (DIV_ROUND_UP(oc, FEATURE_ATOMIC_SIZE) & 1) == 0 ? 0x80011111 : 0x80011011

At oc = 64 that is DIV_ROUND_UP(64, 16) = 4, even, so 0x80011111, so
SIZE_E_2 = 1. Your reading of the merge request line was correct in every
detail; the sentence was mine and it named the arms by the wrong predicate.
Discard it and take the expression. And you are right that we should name the
arms by value and count, so from here: oc = 64 -> 0x80011111 -> SIZE_E_2 = 1.

The vendor toolkit agrees, and I did not have to guess: geom/g_oc64.rknn,
compiled at ic 16, 80x80, k = 5, stride 2, oc = 64, has DPU 0x4050 = 0x80011111.
So do a_lin, a_lin2, a_lin_m1, a_relu, g_pw2 and sv_oc64. pp_oc64 (k = 3,
stride 1) and pw48x64w56 (ic 48, 56x56, k = 1) read 0x80021111, which differs
from the others only in RESERVED_0 - see the last section - and has SIZE_E_2 = 1
as well. Nine vendor models at oc = 64 on this disk, all SIZE_E_2 = 1, across
three kernel sizes, both strides and spatial sizes from 1x1 to 112x112.

Your SIZE_E question, answered from 94 vendor models
====================================================

You asked whether different output channel counts would exercise different
SIZE_E fields, and whether two shapes can rule out one that leans on SIZE_E_1.

I have 94 compiled vendor .rknn on disk from earlier rounds, spanning oc 4 to
1024, ic 3 to 1024, 1x1 to 224x224, k = 1, 3 and 5, stride 1 and 2, regular and
depthwise. Reading DPU 0x4050 out of every one of them and decoding it against
registers.xml:

  SIZE_E_0   = 4     in all 94. It never moves.
  SIZE_E_1   = 0     in all 81 regular models
             = 1     in all 13 depthwise models
  SIZE_E_2   = 0..1  regular, and 0..3 depthwise

So the answer to your question is no, and for a sharper reason than "we have
not seen it move": **SIZE_E_1's axis is the depthwise flag, not the channel
count.** No output channel count can be the shape that leans on SIZE_E_1,
because oc does not select it at all. What does is regular-versus-depthwise,
and the driver already emits a different word entirely on the depthwise path
(0x00013033 | ((DIV_ROUND_UP(oc,16)-1) & 3) << 8), whose SIZE_E_1 is 1, which
is the vendor's depthwise value on all thirteen.

That also upgrades the comment I left in the driver. "SIZE_E_1 left at 0
because two shapes is not every shape" was honest but weaker than the truth: 0
is what the vendor emits on all 81 regular models, and the value upstream
writes there, 1, is what the vendor emits on depthwise. It is not an
unexplained traced constant. I will reword it.

And your padding reading, tested
================================

Your SIZE_E_n + 1 = atoms in the final bank is what the depthwise models say,
taken literally and with the depthwise padding you pointed at - 64 channels,
so the last bank holds one to four atoms:

  oc            16  32  48  64  80  96  112  128  256  1024
  last bank     16  32  48  64  16  32   48   64   64    64
  atoms in it    1   2   3   4   1   2    3    4    4     4
  predicted      0   1   2   3   0   1    2    3    3     3
  observed       0   1   2   3   0   1    2    3    3     3

13 of 13 depthwise models, ten distinct counts, no exceptions - including the
three the driver's comment says it predicted rather than fitted, 16, 80 and 112.
I had been carrying it as (atoms - 1) & 3, which
gets the same numbers and says nothing; your form is the reason for them, and
the mask is just what "the last bank of four" looks like in arithmetic.

On the regular path the last bank is padded to 32 channels, so it holds one atom
or two, which is why only 0 and 1 appear there. Same statement, not a second
rule.

The parity form now has a second, independent confirmation
=========================================================

I scored both candidate readings against all 81 regular models:

  parity, DIV_ROUND_UP(oc,16) even   ->  0 wrong of 81
  modulo, oc % 32 == 0               ->  1 wrong of 81

The one point where they disagree in this corpus is ocp56 - oc = 56, ic = 64,
56x56, k = 1, stride 1 - and the vendor emits 0x80021111, SIZE_E_2 = 1, which is
the parity answer. Every other model in the corpus has an oc where the two forms
agree, which is why the original ten-point sweep could not choose.

That is oc = 56 again, the same count the board picked out as the one shape that
times out under the modulo form. The board and the vendor's own compiler now
agree on the same discriminating count from two directions, which is better
evidence than either alone and better than I claimed at the time.

One thing I cannot explain, flagged rather than claimed
======================================================

RESERVED_0 is 34 in most of the regular models and 66 in a subset of them:
0x80011111 against 0x80021111, which inside the field is its bit 5 against its
bit 6, both over a constant 2. It does not
correlate with oc, ic, spatial size, kernel size or stride: both values appear
at oc 16, 64 and 128, at k 1, 3 and 5, and at both strides. What it does track
is which batch of models it came from, which makes a toolkit setting more likely
than a geometry, but I have not established that. The driver emits 34 always and
the board is correct with it, so this is an open thread rather than a defect I
know about. If you want the table I will send it.

